# 2026-06-07 G39-G44 Hermes / Open WebUI / Docker Autostart Runbook

Use this when Windows native Hermes 0.14, Open WebUI 3000, Docker Desktop,
Telegram bridge, or login autostart need to be checked or repaired on this
machine.

## Final Accepted State

G39 Windows Native Hermes 8642 mainline: PASS.

G42 Docker / WSL2 base: PASS after BIOS firmware virtualization was enabled and
Windows features were corrected.

G43 Open WebUI 3000: PASS.

G44 login autostart: PASS.

2026-06-07 evening follow-up: PASS after adding Docker Desktop residue recovery
to the login autostart script. The user then manually tested the already logged
in Google Chrome Open WebUI page and confirmed it could use Hermes.

Current intended chain:

```text
Windows login
  -> Hermes 8642
  -> Docker Desktop Linux Engine
  -> open-webui-clean
  -> Open WebUI 3000
  -> browser opens http://127.0.0.1:3000
```

## Layer Contract

Do not collapse all layers into one failure.

Stage 00 / G39 Windows Native Hermes 8642 mainline only requires:

- `H:\Hermes_Windows_Native_014` exists.
- `H:\Hermes_Windows_Native_014\launcher\hermes-014-runtime.ps1` exists.
- Hermes CLI exists.
- `C:\Users\A\.hermes` exists.
- port `8642` listens.
- `/health` returns HTTP 200.
- `/v1/models` returns HTTP 200 and contains `hermes-agent`.
- `gateway_state.json` shows `api_server` and, when checking Telegram,
  `telegram` as `connected`.

Do not fail G39 because of BIOS, WSL2, Docker Desktop, `open-webui-clean`, or
Open WebUI `3000`.

Open WebUI `3000` is Stage 01 / G40-G43. It is needed for the web shell, but it
is not a prerequisite for Hermes 8642 or Telegram.

Docker Desktop can be running without the visible Docker Desktop window being
in front. The real proof is:

- `docker-desktop` is `Running` in `wsl -l -v`.
- `docker ps` returns stably.
- `open-webui-clean` is `Up ... (healthy)`.
- `http://127.0.0.1:3000` returns HTTP 200.

Docker Desktop account login is not a requirement for this route. Do not spend
time trying to sign in to Docker Desktop unless the user explicitly asks. The
Open WebUI browser session is the important login state.

## Correct Recovery Order

### G39: Windows Native Hermes 8642

Verify first:

```powershell
curl.exe -s -m 5 http://127.0.0.1:8642/health
$k=(Select-String -LiteralPath 'C:\Users\A\.hermes\.env' -Pattern '^API_SERVER_KEY=' | Select-Object -First 1).Line -replace '^API_SERVER_KEY=',''
curl.exe -s -m 5 -H "Authorization: Bearer $k" http://127.0.0.1:8642/v1/models
Get-Content C:\Users\A\.hermes\gateway_state.json
```

Never print the key. Do not stop Hermes if these checks pass.

Accepted owner pattern:

```text
python.exe ... hermes_cli.main gateway run --replace
```

### G40: Open WebUI 3000 First Pass

Only after G39 passes, check Docker/Open WebUI:

```powershell
sc.exe query com.docker.service
docker version
docker context ls
docker ps -a --filter "name=open-webui-clean"
curl.exe -s -L -m 15 -w "`nHTTP_CODE=%{http_code}`n" http://127.0.0.1:3000
```

If Docker API cannot respond because `docker-desktop` cannot start, stop at:

```text
G40_OPEN_WEBUI_DOCKER_LAYER_FAIL
```

Do not delete containers, volumes, sessions, or Open WebUI data.

### G40b: Docker Desktop Lingering Backend / API 500 Recovery

Use this when the visible Docker Desktop dialog says:

```text
Docker Desktop - Lingering processes detected
com.docker.backend.exe
Failed to connect to Docker Desktop backend
```

or when the command line shows:

```text
docker desktop status -> Status starting
wsl -l -v -> docker-desktop Running
docker ps / docker info -> HTTP 500 on dockerDesktopLinuxEngine
```

This is not a Docker account-login problem. It is a Docker Desktop backend /
WSL handshake problem. The effective non-destructive recovery is:

```powershell
Get-Process | Where-Object {
  $_.ProcessName -like "com.docker.*" -or
  $_.ProcessName -like "Docker*" -or
  $_.ProcessName -in @(
    "docker",
    "docker-compose",
    "docker-credential-desktop",
    "docker-sandbox"
  )
} | ForEach-Object {
  taskkill.exe /PID $_.Id /F /T
}

wsl.exe --terminate docker-desktop
Start-Process "C:\Program Files\Docker\Docker\Docker Desktop.exe"
```

Then wait for:

```powershell
docker ps
docker ps -a --filter "name=^/open-webui-clean$"
docker start open-webui-clean   # only if it exists but is stopped
curl.exe -s -L -m 15 -w "`nHTTP_CODE=%{http_code}`n" http://127.0.0.1:3000
```

Do not use:

```text
wsl --unregister docker-desktop
docker system prune
container/volume deletion
Open WebUI recreation
```

The 2026-06-07 accepted script implementation is in:

```text
H:\Hermes_Windows_Native_014\launcher\auto-start-hermes-openwebui.ps1
```

It defines `Start-DockerDesktop` and `Clear-DockerDesktopResidue`, then uses
this flow:

```text
normal Docker start
-> wait docker ps stable first pass, 240s
-> if it fails, clear Docker Desktop residue
-> terminate only docker-desktop WSL distro
-> start Docker Desktop again
-> wait docker ps stable after residue recovery, 420s
-> start open-webui-clean if stopped
-> wait Open WebUI 3000 HTTP 200
-> open browser to 127.0.0.1:3000
```

Successful evidence from the live repair:

```text
PASS docker ps stable
container status: open-webui-clean|Up ... (health: starting)
PASS Open WebUI 3000 HTTP 200
FINAL_PASS Docker backend and OpenWebUI restored
```

### G41: Docker WSL2 Base Repair

If Docker Desktop Linux Engine fails with `HCS_E_HYPERV_NOT_INSTALLED`, check in
this order:

```powershell
wsl -l -v
docker version
docker context ls
systeminfo | Select-String -Pattern 'Hyper-V Requirements|Virtualization Enabled In Firmware'
dism.exe /online /Get-FeatureInfo /FeatureName:Microsoft-Windows-Subsystem-Linux
dism.exe /online /Get-FeatureInfo /FeatureName:VirtualMachinePlatform
dism.exe /online /Get-FeatureInfo /FeatureName:HypervisorPlatform
dism.exe /online /Get-FeatureInfo /FeatureName:Microsoft-Hyper-V-All
```

Required features for this route:

- `Microsoft-Windows-Subsystem-Linux`: Enabled
- `VirtualMachinePlatform`: Enabled
- `HypervisorPlatform`: Enabled

`Microsoft-Hyper-V-All` can remain Disabled on the non-full-Hyper-V route.

Only enable missing required features. Do not reinstall Docker. Do not run
`wsl --unregister docker-desktop`. Do not run `docker system prune`.

On 2026-06-07, `HypervisorPlatform` was missing and was enabled successfully
with no restart required, but Docker still failed until firmware virtualization
was enabled in BIOS. After BIOS was enabled and Windows rebooted, Docker Desktop
Linux Engine recovered.

### G42: Docker Base Verification After BIOS

Pass evidence:

- `systeminfo` may say `A hypervisor has been detected. Features required for
  Hyper-V will not be displayed.` This is normal after the hypervisor is active.
- `wsl -l -v` shows `docker-desktop Running`.
- `docker version` shows Docker Desktop server and Linux engine.
- `docker info` returns normally.
- `docker ps` returns stably.

Do not require the exact old `Virtualization Enabled In Firmware: Yes` line
after the hypervisor is already running; `systeminfo` changes its output.

### G43: Open WebUI 3000

If `open-webui-clean` exists, do not delete or recreate it. If stopped, start it:

```powershell
docker start open-webui-clean
```

Pass evidence:

- `open-webui-clean` is `Up ... (healthy)`.
- `0.0.0.0:3000->8080/tcp`.
- `http://127.0.0.1:3000` returns HTTP 200.
- page title contains `Open WebUI`.
- Open WebUI config points to `http://host.docker.internal:8642/v1`.
- default model and model IDs include `hermes-agent`.

The Open WebUI API may return `401 Not authenticated` for unauthenticated local
API calls. That is not a failure by itself. Use the local database/config and a
container-to-Hermes model request with a redacted key instead.

When reading Open WebUI config, never print `api_keys`, tokens, passwords, or
secrets. Redact nested JSON strings too.

### G43b: Browser Login And Real Web Use Verification

Do not claim browser success from HTTP 200 alone. HTTP 200 only proves the
server is reachable.

For this user's actual web acceptance, use the already logged-in Google Chrome
window. Do not launch a separate temporary Chromium/Playwright browser and do
not rely on its login state. Temporary browsers will show the Open WebUI login
screen and do not share the user's saved Chrome session.

Accepted web evidence:

- Existing Google Chrome, not a new temporary browser.
- Page title or visible page shows `Open WebUI`.
- The visible model selector/default shows `hermes-agent`.
- The page is not the Open WebUI login form.
- The user can send a message and get a reply, or the user directly confirms
  they just tested the page successfully.

Authenticated backend verification is useful as supporting evidence. Generate a
short-lived local token inside the container without printing it, then call:

```powershell
POST http://127.0.0.1:3000/api/chat/completions
model=hermes-agent
```

This supporting check passed on 2026-06-07 with:

```text
OPENWEBUI_AUTH_CHAT_STATUS=200
MODEL_HERMES=True
HAS_MARKER=True
assistant content: OPENWEBUI_LOGIN_OK
```

But final browser acceptance still belongs to the already logged-in Chrome
session, not the temporary test browser.

### G44: Login Autostart

Task Scheduler task:

```text
Hermes_OpenWebUI_AutoStart
```

Action:

```text
H:\Hermes_Windows_Native_014\AUTO-START-HERMES-OPENWEBUI.cmd
```

Implementation:

```text
H:\Hermes_Windows_Native_014\launcher\auto-start-hermes-openwebui.ps1
```

Required behavior:

1. Confirm/start Hermes 8642.
2. Wait for `/health` HTTP 200.
3. Wait for `/v1/models` to include `hermes-agent`.
4. Confirm/start Docker Desktop.
5. Wait for `docker ps` stable.
6. Confirm/start `open-webui-clean`.
7. Wait for `http://127.0.0.1:3000` HTTP 200.
8. Open browser to `http://127.0.0.1:3000`.

After the 2026-06-07 evening repair, if step 5 times out, the script must run
the Docker Desktop residue recovery described in G40b and then retry Docker
startup once before failing.

Each run writes:

```text
H:\Hermes_Windows_Native_014\reports\autostart-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.log
```

Success line:

```text
FINAL_PASS Windows native Hermes Open WebUI autostart ready
```

Do not make the task use highest privileges unless there is a proven need.
Use "only when user is logged on" / `InteractiveToken`, because Docker Desktop
and browser opening are user-session behavior.

## Mistakes Made During This Incident

1. Docker/WSL/Open WebUI were initially treated as hard prerequisites for
   Windows Native Hermes 8642. That was wrong. G39 8642 mainline can pass
   without Docker or 3000.
2. Long waits such as 10-20 minutes were proposed for a simple startup path.
   That was wrong. The right method is one gate at a time: service, process,
   pipe, WSL distro, Docker API, container, HTTP endpoint.
3. BIOS/virtualization was mentioned too early as a Hermes prerequisite. That
   was wrong for G39. It matters only for Docker Desktop WSL2 Linux Engine.
4. The visible Docker Desktop window was confused with Docker Engine state.
   That was wrong. Engine can run in the background even if the UI is not
   visible.
5. `systeminfo` was initially interpreted too literally. After hypervisor is
   running, it may stop showing `Virtualization Enabled In Firmware: Yes/No` and
   instead show that a hypervisor is detected.
6. A PowerShell heredoc intended for container Python was parsed by PowerShell
   and failed before entering Docker. Use a temporary script file or safe
   quoting for container-side scripts.
7. A config log briefly exposed an Open WebUI API key inside a nested JSON
   string. The log was immediately redacted. Future config readers must redact
   nested JSON strings before writing logs.
8. `schtasks` path quoting failed twice. Use the simplest path when it has no
   spaces, or create a `.cmd` wrapper with a stable path.
9. A temporary Playwright/Chromium browser was opened for web verification.
   That was the wrong browser state for this user. Future verification must use
   the existing logged-in Google Chrome window unless the user explicitly asks
   for an isolated browser.
10. Open WebUI HTTP 200 and an unauthenticated page load were treated as nearly
    sufficient. That is not enough. Browser success requires logged-in UI state
    and a real message/reply test, or the user's immediate confirmation that
    the already logged-in Chrome page works.
11. Docker Desktop visible login/status UI was over-emphasized. Docker account
    login is not required for this route; the required state is background
    Docker Engine availability plus `open-webui-clean` serving Open WebUI.

## Stop Conditions

Stop and report the exact stage instead of crossing layers:

- `G39_HERMES_8642_FAIL`: `/health` or `/v1/models` fail.
- `G40_OPEN_WEBUI_DOCKER_LAYER_FAIL`: Docker API cannot inspect containers.
- `G41_DOCKER_WSL2_ENGINE_BASE_REPAIR_BLOCKED_FIRMWARE_VIRTUALIZATION_OFF`:
  WSL2 Docker backend fails with `HCS_E_HYPERV_NOT_INSTALLED`.
- `OPEN_WEBUI_CONTAINER_NOT_FOUND_NEED_USER_CONFIRM`: `open-webui-clean` is not
  present.
- `NEED_REBOOT`: Windows feature repair requires reboot.

Never delete `open-webui-clean`, Docker volumes, Open WebUI sessions/memory, or
Hermes config as part of these checks.

If the existing Google Chrome window is open but no CDP/debugging port is
available, do not start a separate browser to prove login. Use the screenshot
browser workflow on the existing Chrome window, or use the user's immediate
visible send-test confirmation as acceptance.

